  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is an impeller?",
      "a": "An impeller is a spinning wheel with curved vanes. It grabs water near its centre and throws it outwards, giving the water speed.",
      "asks": [
        "What spins inside a pump?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does water enter a centrifugal pump?",
      "a": "At the eye, the centre of the impeller. The spinning flings water away from the centre, which lowers the pressure there and draws more water in.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does water come in the middle?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the casing do?",
      "a": "The casing, often called a volute, is a spiral that widens towards the outlet. As the fast water slows down in it, its speed turns into pressure.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a volute?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a pump need priming?",
      "a": "An impeller spinning in air cannot fling enough to create suction, because air is so light. So many pumps must be filled with water, or primed, before they start.",
      "asks": [
        "What does priming a pump mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are centrifugal pumps so common?",
      "a": "They are simple, with one spinning part, run smoothly without pulsing, and can move large amounts of water steadily.",
      "asks": [
        "Where are centrifugal pumps used?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A centrifugal pump turns the speed given to water by its impeller into pressure in its widening casing.",
    "Many centrifugal pumps must be primed with water before starting, because spinning air cannot create enough suction."
  ],
